 {
  "name": "alcf-tools__run_pack_kernel",
  "server": "alcf-tools",
  "description": "Run a kernel from a LOCAL codes pack on this facility, end to end\n        (stage the pack + generated runner, submit via IRI, poll, fetch\n        results.json back). Two kinds of packs run this way:\n        (1) a hosted MCP server's kernels \u2014 call its export_dispatch_pack\n        tool first; the app saves the pack locally and reports the path;\n        (2) a locally developed package (e.g. from a coding task): an\n        importable package directory with JSON-serializable entry points \u2014\n        ALWAYS verify it runs locally before dispatching (facility jobs\n        spend allocation and queue time).\n\n        - function: dotted path WITHIN the pack, e.g. 'pk.backends.compute_pk'.\n        - args: JSON object of the kernel's arguments (must be JSON-safe).\n          Its keys are passed as KEYWORD arguments: {\"k\": [...], \"n\": 3}\n          calls function(k=[...], n=3), so the kernel must be\n          `def main(k, n)` - not `def main(args)` (job 59888820 died with\n          \"unexpected keyword argument\" that way).\n        - pack: local directory path of the kernels package (or an importable\n          package name installed on this machine). Directory packs must live\n          inside a research project (\u2026/code/\u2026) or a codebase the user linked\n          to a project \u2014 other host paths are refused.\n        - pip_deps: JSON list or comma-separated pip requirements the kernel\n          needs on the compute node (take them from the pack's manifest).\n        - artifact_dir: local directory for outputs (pass your run\n          directory). Files the kernel wrote remotely are fetched back there,\n          and the kernel's returned arrays are ALSO saved there as\n          <function>_result.json / _result.csv \u2014 use the CSV for local\n          plotting or reports (hosted servers cannot read this machine's\n          files, so never ask them to plot these paths). The accepted job's\n          id/queue/walltime are recorded there as dispatch_<job>.json.\n        - env_lock: a conda-lock file INSIDE the pack (relative path, e.g.\n          'env/conda-lock.yml'). Packs that ship one at that default path are\n          detected automatically: the engine builds a micromamba environment\n          from it on the facility (first job with a new lock: the build is\n          sized by the lock and runs inside a single-node job's walltime, or\n          as a separate 1-node build job ahead of a multi-node job; later\n          jobs reuse the env), so no env_setup / facility environment is needed and\n          pip_deps become optional extras. Pass 'none' to force the plain\n          pip venv path for a pack that has a lock.\n        - duration: requested walltime in seconds \u2014 the real cost driver\n          AND the queue-wait driver: schedulers start short requests first.\n          Size it to the work (the pack manifest's duration_hint_s is a\n          floor, not a target) and continue long work from a checkpoint in a\n          second job rather than asking for a cap. Prompt queues: Perlmutter\n          debug 30 min (<= 8 nodes), then regular up to 48 h; Polaris debug /\n          debug-scaling 60 min (1-10 nodes), prod 3 h at 10-24 nodes, 6 h at\n          25-99. Leave queue empty and the engine picks the queue/QOS that\n          admits the duration; a Polaris job over 60 min below 10 nodes is\n          refused with the choices (shorten + resume, 10+ nodes, or pin\n          queue='capacity' 1-4 nodes/168 h or 'preemptable' 1-10 nodes/72 h,\n          both slow, the latter killable) unless ALCF_PBS_LONG_QUEUE names\n          one; a duration no queue admits is rejected before staging.\n        - nodes / tasks_per_node / cpus_per_task / gpus_per_task: multi-node\n          MPI (contract R9). Only a kernel whose module declares a shape\n          (DISPATCH_MPI, mirrored as `mpi` in the pack manifest) may run on\n          nodes > 1: it then runs as nodes x tasks_per_node MPI ranks, each\n          rank calling the kernel, rank 0's return being the result (the\n          result carries an `mpi` block: ranks, hosts). The three per-task\n          values override the declaration (0 = as declared). A kernel\n          without a declaration is refused at nodes > 1 (the other nodes\n          would idle and still be charged). Cost = nodes x walltime: state\n          the node-hours to the user before calling. The user's node cap\n          (NERSC_MAX_NODES / ALCF_MAX_NODES, see hpc_settings) is enforced\n          before staging; start with the smallest shape that validates\n          (Perlmutter debug <= 8 nodes, Polaris debug <= 2) and scale after.\n          Queues follow the node count (Perlmutter > 8 nodes -> regular;\n          Polaris 3-9 -> debug-scaling within the hour, >= 10 -> prod). On\n          Polaris more nodes for a shorter time beats few nodes for long:\n          10 nodes x 3 h in prod starts far sooner than 2 nodes x 12 h.\n\n        - wait: True (default) blocks until the job finishes and returns its\n          result. False (contract R10) returns as soon as the scheduler\n          accepts the job: {status: 'submitted', job_id, record, next}. Use\n          wait=False for long jobs and whenever more than one job must run at\n          the same time (e.g. one on each facility): submit them all back to\n          back, then poll find_dispatched_jobs(directory=artifact_dir) and\n          fetch each finished job with collect_dispatched_job(record=...).\n          Needs artifact_dir (the record lives there).\n        - image: container carrier (contract R11). Empty = use the pack's own\n          env/image.json when it ships one (the image built from its lock in\n          CI; the kernel then runs inside it - podman-hpc on Perlmutter,\n          Apptainer on Polaris - and no lock env is built on the node);\n          'none' = never use a container; or a fully qualified reference\n          (ghcr.io/org/name:tag or ...@sha256:...) to run in that image. The\n          first job with a new image fetches it (minutes; allowance added to\n          the walltime, or a separate 1-node fetch job ahead of a multi-node\n          job); later jobs start it in seconds. The result's 'carrier' says\n          which path ran. Set DISPATCH_ENV_MODE=lock to disable images.\n\n        One call = one facility job (staging + queue + walltime: minutes to\n        hours). With wait=True the call keeps the connection alive with\n        progress heartbeats while the job is queued/running. On a structured\n        error, do NOT retry \u2014 surface it to the user.",
